This pair is asked in nearly every JS interview, and the fix is entirely explained by the scope-unit row above:

```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// logs: 3, 3, 3

for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 0);
}
// logs: 0, 1, 2
```

With `var`, there is exactly **one** `i`, function-scoped to the whole loop. All three callbacks close over the same variable, and by the time any of them runs (after the loop has finished), `i` is `3`.

With `let`, the spec gives the loop a **fresh binding of `j` per iteration** — each callback closes over its own copy, holding the value that iteration had. No `var` even exists at block scope, so there's nothing to share.
